<document source="adrs/ADR-004-websocket-transport.md" type="adr" repo="re-cinq/the-expert-ui" relevance="1.00" tokens="467">
## Alternatives Considered

### Keep `POST /answer` via a backend compatibility shim

Rejected. The HAL-engine entry protocol streams partial state (`entry_upsert` events) the REST shape can't express. Maintaining a shim would freeze us out of any future streaming benefits and double the per-turn round-trips.

### Use a third-party library (e.g. `socket.io`, `pusher-js`, `phoenix`)

Rejected. The HAL protocol is a thin, well-defined JSON-over-WS message set. None of these libraries' added features (rooms, presence, broadcast) apply, and each would force a backend-side adapter we don't need.

### Manage the connection from the route, not the component

Rejected for now. Tests render the component standalone and would otherwise need a custom test harness to drive route hooks. The component already owns its message-handling state (the local `TrackedArray`), so co-locating connection lifecycle keeps the boundary clean.

## Consequences

### Positive

- Backend and frontend share one transport contract; the FE is now a real consumer of the HAL protocol, exercising it end to end.
- Each turn requires one WS round-trip plus one `GET /chats/:id` instead of one HTTP POST + one GET. Same network footprint, lower perceived latency once we add streaming chunks in the future.
- The pure `sessionEntryToHistory` helper and `MockWebSocket` test harness are reusable for any future component that consumes the same protocol.

### Negative / accepted trade-offs

- "Question N of M" label briefly disappears between WS push and `stream_end` reload. Acceptable UX; documented.
- No retry on reconnect failure beyond one shot. Acceptable for the chat-session lifetime; would need revisiting if we add long-lived rooms or admin live-views.
- Test infrastructure now has two mock layers (Pretender for HTTP, MockWebSocket for WS). Adds a small mental tax for new tests.
</document>
</section>
</context>

